reat  the crazy balancing act to pull off that  will lead both of them to a lot of  incidental cooperation as it did during  the suck another interesting thing to  note about yuji in particular though is  that he isn't anywhere near as flat of a  character as he may have originally  seemed he basically gave me exactly what  i wanted from a continuation  and what i mean by this is that i'm sure  you know the whole classic protagonist  trope of the insanely driven young boy  who never gives up  no matter what the thing is well  something quite pleasantly surprising  about yuji in this arc is that he has a  pretty extreme inner conflict tossing up  his ideas of pain and fear versus his  mission to quote help everyone there's  this very genuine struggle of self and  it makes yugi far more relatable and i  would say  far more human there is this side to him  that simply isn't  cool if you will which if anything makes  his ultimate resolve that much more  impressive and speaking of that actually  leads us into the cliffhanger of the  previous arc actually where it was  stated that one of our precious first  years would die and as suspected  it was eugene although i'm nowhere near  as annoyed with this as i thought i  might be given the scenario that was set  up i do think that yuji's decision to  sacrifice himself was very well made  although i'll reserve for judgement  until his inevitable revival has been  completed because usually i despise  using death or a death scene in any  series where the character is later  revealed to be alive or revived but  jiujitsukai sin lands in a very  interesting area given that the major  motif of the series at this point is  death it actually becomes far more  natural and more welcome to delve into  it because i suppose a big problem with  death is that usually if it's used well  within a story  there's actually surprisingly little to  explore all you have are the lingering  effects of death on those left behind  but it looks like yuji is about to  confront this concept in a more  head-on manner which is quite exciting  and that is where i left off by the way  i got to the end of chapter 10 which is  yugi smack talking sukuna in the very um  corpsey realm and i'm quite excited to  see where that goes but our other  major focus of this arc would be  fushiguru and rather wonderfully i got a  bit of what i was looking for last week  with some personality expansion and in  fact i got a bit more than what i  bargained for because we've got some  pretty big questions coming from his  general direction  such as who's to say someone you save  won't kill someone in the future as well  as the whole theoretical concept that  karma doesn't happen on its own it's a  more active process  at least according to fushiguru all of  which is quite fascinating because of  course most of his philosophy is very  much at odds with his decision to save  yuji in the first time something that is  resolved via epiphany of just wanting to  save yuji because he's a good person  regardless of the world ending evil  residing within him so fushiguro is  actually a bit of a complicated guy with  a clear sense of his own moral values i  did notice that he didn't come to the  conclusion of wanting to help everyone  though as yuji does his personality  didn't actually change as a result of  this arc which is fascinating and he  still wishes to help only those whom he  deems good which sounds like it could  lead to some fun potential conflict in  the future there was also some potential  seeding for his father as well like  there was this whole weird line that  went something like well my father  didn't even know my gender but he's  still alive  out there somewhere a latter of which  honestly seems like a pretty pointless  thing to say unless he's going to appear  at some stage in the future  such family drama i'm also quite  enamored by his abilities to create  doggos froggos and even a bigger mask  bird although i am annoyed that t